WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.802
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:08.460
SPEAKER: Google Cloud enables
you to migrate virtual machine

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:13.140
instances, images, or disks
from on premise environments

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:16.230
or other Cloud providers
to Compute Engine.

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:19.740
There are several ways
you can migrate workloads

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:24.240
to Compute Engine, including
migrate for Compute Engine,

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:29.220
importing virtual appliances,
or importing virtual disks.

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:31.890
In this video, we
will focus on how

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:36.560
to import virtual appliances
and virtual disks.

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:39.620
Migrating virtual
appliances and virtual disks

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:42.620
is best suited for
small number of VMs.

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:45.320
If you want to migrate
a large number of VMs,

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:48.050
check out Migrate
for Compute Engine.

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:51.800
The first scenario will cover
importing virtual appliances,

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:54.870
followed with virtual disks.

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:57.210
Virtual appliance
is a package that

00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:59.670
contains hardware
configuration and disk

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:02.730
images for a VM instance.

00:01:02.730 --> 00:01:04.530
Before you start
a migration, you

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:08.880
should always check if the OS
is supported for migration,

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:11.550
check if the OS is
compatible for migration

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:14.190
by running the
precheck tool, choose

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:16.500
if you will use your
own license keys

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:21.090
or if you will use Google
Cloud's pay as you go model,

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:24.570
set up a storage bucket,
grant IAM permissions

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:27.360
for the import operation.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:29.850
After we complete the
migration prerequisites,

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:32.500
we will do one of
the following--

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:36.300
either export the VMs
into OVA or OVF format

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:39.960
and upload them to a
GCS bucket, or export

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:42.510
the virtual disks,
which are in VMDK

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:47.760
or VHD format to a GCS bucket.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:51.570
Check out this documentation for
more information on uploading

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:53.870
files to a GCS bucket.

00:01:53.870 --> 00:01:55.820
For this example,
we have already

00:01:55.820 --> 00:02:01.130
uploaded an OVF package and
one good disk to a bucket.

00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:05.510
Next, we import the OVF
files into a Compute Engine.

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:09.740
We can import machine images
through a gcloud command

00:02:09.740 --> 00:02:12.500
or a Compute Engine API.

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:15.290
We have two options
when importing.

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:17.990
We can either create
an instance directly

00:02:17.990 --> 00:02:22.290
while importing the OVF, or
import it as a machine image,

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:26.340
then create a new
instance from that image.

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:29.820
The command on the screen
imports the virtual appliance

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:32.380
directly into an instance.

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:36.750
The source URI here is the
path to the OVF directory.

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:42.830
There you go.

00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:45.760
We have successfully
imported an instance

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:48.170
from a virtual appliance.

00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:51.590
The command shown here
creates a machine image

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:55.520
from this virtual appliances,
and these machine images

00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:59.540
can be used to create
instances later on.

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:03.140
There are many flags
available for custom settings,

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:09.230
such as byol to specify
bring your own license model,

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:14.240
project to specify the project
you want to import this image,

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:19.160
zone to specify the zone in
which image to be imported,

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:24.740
guest OS features to enable
one or more supported features.

00:03:24.740 --> 00:03:28.250
You can explore more of these
flags in the documentation.

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:33.420
There you go.

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:36.410
We have successfully
imported the machine image

00:03:36.410 --> 00:03:38.450
from the virtual appliance.

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:42.920
Next we will cover how
to import virtual disks.

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:47.810
Virtual disk is a file used by
virtual machines that emulate

00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:50.420
an actual disk storage device.

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:53.360
Migration prerequisites
remains the same

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:56.130
for importing virtual disks.

00:03:56.130 --> 00:03:58.740
We can import
virtual disks using

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:03.480
Google Cloud console, Google
Cloud CLI, and Compute Engine

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:04.620
API.

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:09.090
In Google Cloud console,
go to Create an Image page.

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:12.210
Specify a name of the
image and a source.

00:04:12.210 --> 00:04:17.810
Select virtual disk, browse
the virtual disk file,

00:04:17.810 --> 00:04:20.060
specify the
operating system that

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:23.300
is available in
the imported disk,

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:25.775
then click Create
to import the image.

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:30.260
Congratulations.

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:35.480
We have successfully created
an image from the virtual disk.

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:39.440
Check out the documentation to
learn more about Compute Engine

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:41.900
migration parts.

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:44.950
[MUSIC PLAYING]

